Five legal judgments:
Altering official documents by adding or deleting text;
Unlawfully establishing a principal son as heir;
Buddhist monks and Daoist priests bowing to their parents;
A commanding general failing to hold his position;
Failure to accept and hear a complaint.

Third Session
Five policy questions

Question: Where there is an extraordinary ruler, there must be extraordinary institutions and compositions; where there is conduct of the highest virtue, there must be writings of the highest virtue. The canons, counsels, instructions, announcements, oaths, and charges—the institutions and writings of Tang and Yu and the Three Dynasties—were excellent indeed. We reverently consider how our Emperor Gao, Taizu, compiled the Grand Pronouncements (Da Gao) in three collections; Emperor Wen, Taizong, produced the book Nature and Principle (Xingli); and Emperor Zhang, Xuanzong, produced the book Five Human Relationships (Wulun). These surely follow the same course and accord with the institutions and writings of Tang and Yu and the Three Dynasties. Humbly reading what is recorded in the Pronouncements, we find passages called “Ruler and Ministers Touring Together” and “Elucidating the Five Constant Virtues,” and others called “The People of the Capital Rejoicing Together” and “Severing Fingers and Slandering.” Compared with what the canons and counsels …
